
# u-boot for imx93_phyboard_nash

## Build targets

u-boot is the bootloader used for this board, it actually consist
of different boot stages and firmware assemble into a boot container,
usually named 'imx-boot' or 'flash.bin'.

A boot container usually consist of:
 - ATF (arm trusted firmware)
 - OP-TEE (optional: usually not provided)
 - u-boot
 - firmware for DDR training
 - firmware for secure-element (firmware-ele)

The boot container is put together at the last building stage either
using mkimage or binman.

u-boot for this board is provided as a port, the first step is to prepare
the port using the Genode's build system:

```bash
prepare_port imx93_phyboard_nash_uboot
```

Different build configuration are provided in the 'spec/' subfolder:
 - dev
 - staging

## dev configuration

This build configuration is typically used by developer for fast development
cycle and on CI/CD.

First build the boot container, assuming you are in `genode/`:

```bash
make -C build/arm_v8a u-boot/imx93_phyboard_nash/spec/dev
```

The resulting image will be available here in the build directory
`build/arm_v8a/u-boot/imx93_phyboard_nash/spec/dev/imx-boot`.

### run with uuu

First set the bootmode switch of the board to USB serial download:

| ON |   |   |   |
|----|---|---|---|
| x  |   |   |   |
|    | x | x | x |
| 1  | 2 | 3 | 4 |

You can now from your host use uuu [1] to upload the boot container to the
device and run it, independently of what is install on the device's emmc/sd-card.

[1]: https://github.com/nxp-imx/mfgtools

```bash
uuu -v -b spl build/arm_v8a/u-boot/imx93_phyboard_nash/spec/dev/imx-boot
```

When you will power the board on, it will now download the newly built boot-container,
en run it. By default it will run `fastboot usb 0`. You can change this behavior by
editing the `bootcmd_mfg` env variable in the default env `src/u-boot/imx93_phyboard_nash/spec/dev/dev.env`.
Keep in mind, in this mode, the u-boot's ENV is not read from emmc, it is always loaded
from nowhere, read u-boot documentation for more details.

You can also access the u-boot shell via serial line by typing `CTRL+C`.

### flash to emmc with uuu

First set the boot-mode switch of the board to USB serial download:

| ON |   |   |   |
|----|---|---|---|
| x  |   |   |   |
|    | x | x | x |
| 1  | 2 | 3 | 4 |

You can now from your host use uuu [1] to upload the boot container to the
device, it will be flashed to emmc.

[1]: https://github.com/nxp-imx/mfgtools

```bash
uuu -v -b emmc build/arm_v8a/u-boot/imx93_phyboard_nash/spec/dev/imx-boot
```

Finally, set the boot-mode switch of the board to emmc:

| ON |   |   |   |
|----|---|---|---|
|    | x |   |   |
| x  |   | x | x |
| 1  | 2 | 3 | 4 |

## staging configuration

This build configuration is typically used for pre-production and production
environment. The major difference with the dev configuration is the expectation
of a device with restrained access to the i/o serial console, with burned fused
and locked state.

## Epoch

For build reproducibility, we set a fix epoch during the build process in
`src/u-boot/imx93_phyboad_nash/epoch.env`. The epoch is typically updated
after major change. The epoch is obtained with the following command

```bash
date +%s
```
